[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
The campus access network and switch ports serving Laptop2 and Laptop3 are operating normally. Both laptops are on the same subnet (172.16.10.0/24) with eth0 up, and direct L3/L2 connectivity between them is healthy: Laptop2 (172.16.10.12) can ping Laptop3 (172.16.10.13) and vice versa with 0% loss and low RTTs. This proves ARP resolution, Layer-2 forwarding on switch1 (ports fa3_1 and fa4_1), and Layer-3 IP reachability are intact.

Mechanism: If the office switch or either access port were down/misconfigured (wrong VLAN, err-disable, etc.), we would see failed ARP or ICMP loss. Instead, successful bidirectional ICMP indicates frames traverse the same collision domain/VLAN without issue.

Localization: Endpoints laptop2 and laptop3, interfaces eth0; access aggregation via switch1. No evidence of faults on mswitch1/switch1 or router uplinks.

Root cause: The reported “can’t see each other” issue is not caused by the network; L2/L3 connectivity between Laptop2 and Laptop3 is normal at this time.

Corrective fix: Engage endpoint/application support to verify host sharing/discovery. Specifically: ensure file-sharing services are enabled and listening (SMB/NetBIOS/mDNS), local firewalls allow TCP/445 and UDP 137–138/5353/5355, and try accessing by IP (e.g., \\172.16.10.13\share from Laptop2). If name discovery is required, confirm LLMNR/mDNS settings. From the network side, no changes are required.
=== END ===